---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DecisionCard from "../../components/DecisionCard.astro";
import SystemMap from "../../components/SystemMap.astro";
import { cases, decisions } from "../../data/profile";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const item = cases.find((caseItem) => caseItem.slug === "omni-agent")!;
---
<BaseLayout title="Omni-Agent" description="Omni-Agent case study by Tran Quoc Truong." >
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="page-kicker">
        <span class="tag">{item.label}</span>
        <span class="tag">{item.role}</span>
      </div>
      <h1>Omni-Agent: enterprise AI platform for Agentic QA and document intelligence.</h1>
      <p class="page-summary">{item.summary}</p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn primary" href="https://github.com/truong51972" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View GitHub</a>
        <a class="btn" href={`${base}projects/`}>Back to projects</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section tight">
    <div class="container content-grid">
      <article class="article">
        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Context</div>
          <h2>The problem is not just RAG. It is governing the workflow around RAG.</h2>
          <p>Omni-Agent is designed as an enterprise console where Knowledge Builder and Agentic QA workflows can live under project-level boundaries. The hard part is not only calling an LLM. The hard part is making documents, versions, artifacts, jobs, chunks, vector records, and evaluation traces behave like a reliable product surface.</p>
          <p>The case is intentionally framed as a backend control-plane project: Django owns domain state, worker services perform technical jobs, and result envelopes return evidence that can be stored, reviewed, retried, or indexed.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Architecture</div>
          <h2>Control plane, workers, artifacts, and retrieval are separated.</h2>
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="diagram-step"><b>Project console</b><span>React UI for documents, versions, preview, jobs, and review actions.</span></div>
            <div class="diagram-step"><b>Django API</b><span>Domain models, permissions, jobs, source-of-truth state, and presigned artifact access.</span></div>
            <div class="diagram-step"><b>Stateless workers</b><span>OCR, chunking, document conversion, and indexing commands via queue/result envelopes.</span></div>
            <div class="diagram-step"><b>RAG layer</b><span>Chunks, vector DB sync, retrieval traces, and Agentic QA workflows.</span></div>
          </div>
          <p>The important boundary is that technical workers do not secretly own product state. They receive commands and return results; the API decides what a DocumentVersion means and when it becomes committed.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Core modeling</div>
          <h2>DocumentVersion and ProcessingArtifact make AI output auditable.</h2>
          <p>The document flow is modeled around versions and artifacts: source file, OCR markdown, parsed document JSON, enhanced OCR, committed text, chunk artifacts, and chunk-version mappings. This makes RAG traceability easier because every retrieval unit can be connected back to a committed document version.</p>
          <p>This model is more verbose than a simple “upload file → embed text” flow, but it gives better control over review, rollback, preview, reprocessing, and debugging.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Product decisions</div>
          <h2>Engineering decisions worth discussing in interviews.</h2>
          <div class="decision-grid">
            {decisions.slice(0, 3).map((decision) => <DecisionCard item={decision} />)}
          </div>
        </section>
      </article>

      <aside class="sidebar">
        <SystemMap compact />
        <div class="side-card">
          <h3>Stack</h3>
          <div class="tag-row" style="padding-top:0">
            {item.tags.map((tag) => <span class="tag">{tag}</span>)}
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="side-card">
          <h3>Case proof</h3>
          <p>{item.outcome}</p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
